

🧠 Key Principle

You only rerun detection when it’s necessary.
Necessity arises from:

1. Scene Change: UI/layout/scene content has changed.


2. Gaze Change: User starts gazing in a new region.


3. Fixation Confirmed: Gaze is stable long enough to consider a target.


4. Temporal Threshold: Detection is considered stale after N frames.




---

🎯 Gaze-Based Object Detection Flow

Step-by-step:

1. Initial Frame

Run full edge or object detection.

Store results (segment texture / bounding boxes / contours).

Wait for fixation.



2. Frame-by-Frame

If fixation is within a previously detected object → ✅ Reuse.

If gaze is wandering → ❌ Don't rerun detection yet.

If gaze enters a new region AND stays for N frames → 🔁 Rerun detection in that region only (ROI).

If scene changes (e.g. UI refreshed) → 🔁 Rerun full detection.



3. Trigger Conditions

Fixation stabilized in new area.

Scene/UI invalidated (manual flag or checksum diff).

Gaze out of bounds from known target.





---

✅ When to Rerun Detection (per method)

Method	Rerun When...

Pixel color ID map	Only when scene changes or new objects appear.
Bounding box reuse	Rerun if gaze exits all known boxes for N frames.
ROI processing	Rerun in that region when gaze moves and stabilizes.
Contour tracking	Rerun if shapes drift or object identity becomes unclear.
Fixation buffer	Triggers detection only when fixation occurs.
Segment texture caching	Only rerun if hash or checksum of image frame changes.
Frame delta check	Detects subtle changes → rerun detection if diff is high.



---

🔁 Re-run Strategy

Frame	Gaze Stable?	Scene Changed?	Detection Action

1	No	No	Reuse previous data
2	No	No	Reuse
3	Yes (in ROI)	No	Rerun in ROI
10	Yes (new UI)	Yes	Rerun full detection



---

🛠 Tip for Unity Integration

Use gaze fixation logic (e.g., >300ms at same pixel region) before invoking native CV.

Compare current camera-rendered buffer hash or mean difference before rerunning detection.

Maintain a target history with a TTL (Time To Live) that forces recheck every N frames if untouched
